    "md5": "4c4400d8bd734bf5f309a521ced458d5",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1899,
    "chunk_text": "In many ways, Uthpala felt like a fish out of water. Many of his peers were from privileged, Westernized, and highly sophisticated backgrounds. He was a product of a very different environment. Though he had reached the top, there was still a gulf between his world and theirs. Perhaps it was this which caught my eye. Whatever the reason, in 2023, when I began my research career, it was Uthpala who became my first assistant.\n\nWhenever we were free, the two of us would discuss our future and debate history, art, culture, society, politics, and other topics. By now he had enrolled at an international relations diploma program and was studying for his entrance exams at the Sri Lanka Law College. By February the next year, he had finished part of the international relations program and joined Law College. He had also qualified for a government university, the University of Peradeniya, which lay several hundred miles from Colombo.\n\nIt was during these conversations that he began to think about a research project of his own. Though he was focused on a career in law, he remained interested in culture and history. In January 2025, he collected his thoughts and put them together for his application to the OSF. Two months later, he was confirmed as a Fellow.\n\nBorn and raised in a village and educated in a city, Uthpala confronted multiple worlds and immersed himself in them all. This gave him a foundation for his thinking. Though diligent, he began to question what he was studying.\n\nIt is this which ultimately informed his project, which is simply titled Five Sacred Sites. As the title makes clear, it explores the history of five sites. Two of these are in Kurunegala, in the northwestern province, while three are in the north.\n\nWhen pondering his choices, Uthpala notes that he wanted to focus on sites far away from locations that scholars of Sri Lankan history usually focus on."
  },
  {
