    "description": "Kerala: Left Falls, Not Out",
    "url_metadata": "http://island.lk/kerala-left-falls-not-out/",
    "lang": "en",
    "newspaper_id": "islandlk",
    "time_ut": 1778945820.0,
    "chunk_id": "2026-05-16-islandlk-543d1f6e-0002",
    "chunk_index": 2,
    "language": "en",
    "md5": "7cca35b3271cc771cd9c832441ccf5fd",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1764,
    "chunk_text": "Kerala followed a different path. But constitutional integration comes with consequences. Once a movement stops functioning as an opposition force and becomes part of governance, it begins to absorb the logic of administration. The CPI(M) in Kerala became a tightly organized electoral machine, but over time that discipline turned into rigidity. Governance replaced transformation, welfare delivery replaced structural change, and political conviction itself shifted from practice to narrative.\n\nYet this shift takes place within a structurally strained system. At an India-focused academic conference held at Stanford University last week, Shashi Tharoor made a blunt observation: if Kerala were treated as a sovereign fiscal entity, it would already be considered effectively bankrupt.\n\nDespite its high human development indicators and global reputation for social welfare, Kerala\u2019s fiscal model is heavily dependent on borrowing, remittances, and central transfers. Salary obligations for government employees, pension liabilities, and welfare commitments increasingly operate under tight fiscal constraints. Political promises continue to expand while revenue growth remains structurally limited. The result is not collapse, but chronic imbalance.\n\nThis fiscal tension matters because Kerala\u2019s politics is heavily driven by welfare expectations. Every electoral cycle expands commitments without fully resolving the question of long-term sustainability. Political parties, regardless of orientation, operate within this constraint. The CPI(M) expands welfare schemes under Left governance, while the Congress-led UDF offers competing promises of welfare expansion and subsidy re-calibration. Political identity becomes secondary to distributive capacity."
  },
