    "md5": "ac74a63ff2a1b462a24fac6db4370e2b",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1761,
    "chunk_text": "Over time, however, even that moral and social foundation has been absorbed into routine political language. All major parties now invoke equality, welfare, and inclusion, but these terms function as electoral jargon rather than transformative principles. The Congress operates through alliance management and electoral calibration rather than coherent political positioning. The CPI(M) governs through administrative efficiency and welfare continuity rather than mass mobilization.\n\nWhat emerges is a political system that is stable but intellectually exhausted. The red flag in Kerala has not disappeared, but it has been normalized. It no longer signals rupture; it signals continuity. The revolutionary vocabulary that once defined the CPI(M) has been absorbed into the grammar of governance. Even electoral victories are framed in managerial terms rather than political triumph.\n\nThe recent electoral outcome and leadership transition reflect this transformation clearly. Satheesan\u2019s rise to Cliff House, the official residence of the Kerala Chief Minister, is not the emergence of a new political direction but the consolidation of a performance-based governing model. Leadership is now judged by electoral execution, alliance management, and narrative control rather than doctrinal articulation. Venugopal\u2019s organizational strength, Chennithala\u2019s seniority, and Satheesan\u2019s electoral visibility represent three competing logics within the same exhausted system. Meanwhile, the current CPI(M) leadership in Kerala is still shaped by the post-2000 consolidation phase, where administrative governance has increasingly replaced mass mobilization. The fact that visibility prevailed does not signal renewal; it signals adaptation to voter psychology."
