    "chunk_text": "Colombo Dockyard which was a regional player and has been building ships and yachts for Europe and earning dollars \u2013 will it be now reduced to repairing dredging vessels and being paid in Indian rupees? The currency exchange scheme with India apparently is still one way, that is Indians can pay Indian rupees for goods and services in Sri Lanka but it seems Sri Lankans cannot pay for goods and services in India with Sri Lankan rupees. Direct currency exchange agreements are useful when reciprocal, avoiding the costly intermediary of the US dollar, an example being the old Asian Currency Union where Sri Lanka is able to settle outstanding balances by the end of the month.\n\nDoes the final solution lie in our building consensus on domestic policy despite the inherited system of confrontational politics? Our Nordic friends once pointed to the seating in our parliament which pits the Government against the Opposition, in contrast with the Nordic tradition of seating members according to the regions to promote cooperation within the region and planning together for the future. In this context, the recent decision to appoint a leading member of the Opposition as head of the Cricket Board is welcome. The new Board will be tested by the legal challenges to building a cricket stadium for Jaffna on Nanditivu island. This should be re-located to save Nanditivu\u2019s pristine environment, special biodiversity and people\u2019s traditional livelihoods.\n\n(Sarala Fernando retired from the Foreign Ministry as Additional Secretary, her last Ambassadorial appointment was as Permanent Representative to the UN and International Organizations in Geneva. Her Ph.D was on India-Sri Lanka relations and she writes now on foreign policy, public diplomacy and protection of heritage).\n\nLeave a Reply Cancel replyYour email address will not be published. Required fields are marked *Comment * Name *\n\nSave my name, email, and website in this browser for the next time I comment."
